{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication mismatch — selecting a drug that treats a symptom or comorbidity rather than the stated primary diagnosis; (2) safety/contraindication neglect — ignoring an active patient-specific risk (e.g., seizure history, active GI bleeding, current immunosuppression) when a safer same-class option exists; (3) option-set discrimination — failing to distinguish near-duplicate or same-class options (e.g., two NSAIDs, two beta-blockers, two estrogens) by route, formulation, or guideline preference. A fourth, weaker mechanism is over-weighting a current medication's theoretical interaction while under-weighting the primary indication.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "anchor_primary_diagnosis",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the single primary diagnosis the encounter is treating. Eliminate any option whose approved indication does not match that diagnosis, even if it plausibly treats a comorbidity or symptom mentioned in the history. Only among indication-matching options proceed to safety and preference ranking."
    },
    {
      "id": "active_risk_screen",
      "text": "Extract every stated active risk factor (e.g., seizure disorder, active bleeding, immunosuppression, recent sunburn, organ impairment) and require the chosen option to be compatible with each. If two options share an indication, prefer the one with the lower systemic exposure or better-documented safety for the stated risk; do not assume a risk is absent because it was not asked about."
    },
    {
      "id": "same_class_tiebreak",
      "text": "When multiple options belong to the same drug class or are near-duplicates, break the tie using route of administration, systemic absorption, guideline first-line status for the specific indication, and dosing reliability in the patient's current state. Do not treat same-class agents as interchangeable."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_interaction_balance",
      "text": "Treat a current medication as a modifier, not a disqualifier: first confirm the candidate still matches the primary indication, then check interaction/additive risk with the current regimen. Do not let a theoretical interaction override a clear indication match unless a safer indication-matching alternative exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Never supply a patient fact (allergy, lab value, contraindication, prior therapy response) that was not stated. If a safety determination depends on an unstated fact, note the uncertainty and choose the option that is safest under the stated facts rather than assuming the missing fact is favorable."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "primary diagnosis match",
    "active risk factor",
    "same-class tiebreak",
    "route of administration",
    "systemic absorption",
    "first-line for indication",
    "current medication interaction",
    "unstated fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated primary diagnosis, the current medicines, and every explicitly stated risk factor or comorbidity. Separate facts that were stated from facts that were not asked about; never fill a gap with an assumption.",
    "safety_check": "Screen the indication-matching candidates against each stated active risk factor and against the current regimen. Prefer lower systemic exposure or better-documented safety when risks are present; flag unstated facts as uncertainty rather than treating them as absent or present.",
    "option_selection": "Rank only options that match the primary diagnosis. Among them, apply safety compatibility with stated risks, then same-class tiebreaks (route, absorption, guideline first-line status, dosing reliability), then interaction balance with current medicines."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the named current medication and its class, treating it as the target of the question or as a reason to avoid otherwise indicated options. It also fails to distinguish 'already failed/ineffective' from 'contraindicated', and it sometimes selects a drug for a secondary symptom rather than the primary diagnosis. Missing facts (e.g., no explicit interaction) are sometimes treated as present safety barriers, while real safety signals (bleeding history, adherence difficulty, respiratory suppression) are inconsistently applied.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "primary_indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the single most likely active diagnosis from the stated symptoms and history. Eliminate any option that does not treat that primary diagnosis, even if it treats a secondary symptom (e.g., headache, hypertension, pain). Only after indication is satisfied should safety and patient-specific factors break ties."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_is_context_not_target",
      "text": "A medication the patient is already taking is usually context for interaction/safety checks, not the treatment target. Do not select an option merely because it belongs to the same class as the current medication, and do not avoid an indicated option merely because the patient is on another drug unless a specific interaction or contraindication is stated or well established."
    },
    {
      "id": "failed_vs_contraindicated",
      "text": "Distinguish 'already tried and ineffective' from 'unsafe.' If the patient reports a current medicine is not working well enough, that medicine is a weak choice for the same indication, but it is not automatically contraindicated. Prefer a different indicated agent when one exists; if none exists, reconsider whether the current medicine can be optimized rather than replaced."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_signal_extraction",
      "text": "Explicitly extract patient-specific safety signals before choosing: bleeding history or anticoagulant/antiplatelet use, adherence difficulty or need for monitoring, respiratory suppression risk, organ impairment, pregnancy, and age. Use these to eliminate options only when the signal creates a real contraindication or a clearly worse risk-benefit profile, not when the fact is merely absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "Do not treat an unstated interaction, allergy, contraindication, or patient preference as if it were present. Conversely, do not assume safety from silence: if an option is otherwise indicated and no stated fact rules it out, it remains a candidate. Base elimination on stated facts or well-established pharmacology, not on speculation."
    },
    {
      "id": "adherence_and_monitoring_fit",
      "text": "When the patient reports difficulty adhering to strict schedules or frequent monitoring, prefer an indicated option with a simpler regimen or less monitoring burden over one requiring tight control, provided both treat the primary diagnosis. Do not let adherence concerns override indication entirely; they are tie-breakers among indicated options."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "primary diagnosis",
    "current medication as context",
    "ineffective versus contraindicated",
    "patient-specific safety signal",
    "missing fact not present",
    "adherence and monitoring burden"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated primary condition, the current medicines, and any explicit patient-specific facts (bleeding history, adherence difficulty, respiratory risk, organ function). Separate facts that are stated from facts that are merely absent.",
    "safety_check": "Check only stated or well-established contraindications and interactions. Do not invent a missing fact as a barrier, and do not ignore a stated safety signal. Distinguish 'already ineffective' from 'unsafe.'",
    "option_selection": "First require that the option treats the primary diagnosis. Then use patient-specific safety and adherence factors to choose among indicated options. Do not select for a secondary symptom or for class-matching with a current medicine."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the patient's current medication as a reason to avoid or deprioritize the correct drug, and under-weights the primary diagnosis when selecting among options. Failures cluster into: (1) misreading 'already on drug X' as a contraindication to X or as a reason to pick a different class, when the correct action is often to continue/add the same or complementary agent; (2) choosing an option based on a secondary comorbidity rather than the active diagnosis; (3) treating a benign concurrent medication as a major interaction without evidence; (4) selecting a drug that is not indicated for the diagnosed condition because it sounds related to a symptom.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_first_selection",
      "text": "When choosing among medication options, first anchor on the active diagnosis stated in the case. Eliminate options that are not indicated for that diagnosis before considering patient-specific modifiers. Do not let a secondary or historical condition override the primary indication unless it creates a true contraindication."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_not_contraindication",
      "text": "If the patient is already taking a medication that is also among the options, do not automatically exclude it or assume it is contraindicated. Determine whether the question is asking for continuation, dose adjustment, or an additional/complementary agent. 'Already on X' is not by itself a reason to avoid X."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_requires_evidence",
      "text": "Only treat a concurrent medication as a clinically significant interaction if there is a known, specific interaction relevant to the candidate drug. Do not invent interactions from general statements like 'we should be aware of potential interactions.' Benign supplements (e.g., vitamin C, iron) and unrelated analgesics should not drive option selection absent a real interaction."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_over_symptom_match",
      "text": "Match each option to the diagnosed condition's standard treatment, not merely to a symptom word. An option that shares a symptom or organ system with the presentation but lacks indication for the diagnosis should be eliminated. Prefer first-line or well-supported agents for the specific diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_check_patient_specific",
      "text": "After indication-based filtering, check the remaining options against the patient's stated comorbidities, allergies, and current medicines. Eliminate only those with a documented patient-specific contraindication or a clinically meaningful interaction. If no such contraindication exists, do not eliminate an otherwise indicated option."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_benchmark_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not use answer letters, case identifiers, or positional patterns to select options. Base every choice on the stated diagnosis, indication, and patient-specific safety facts present in the dialogue. If a needed fact is missing, do not assume it is present."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "active diagnosis",
    "indicated for diagnosis",
    "already taking",
    "continuation vs addition",
    "clinically significant interaction",
    "patient-specific contraindication",
    "first-line treatment",
    "symptom vs indication"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the active diagnosis, the current medication list, and any stated comorbidities or allergies. Distinguish the primary condition being treated from historical or secondary conditions. Do not infer facts that are not stated.",
    "safety_check": "Check only documented, specific contraindications and interactions for the candidate drugs. Do not treat a benign or unrelated concurrent medication as a barrier, and do not treat 'already on drug X' as a contraindication to X.",
    "option_selection": "Filter options by indication for the active diagnosis first, then apply patient-specific safety. Prefer first-line or well-supported agents. Do not use answer letters, case IDs, or positional shortcuts."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication mismatch — selecting an agent that does not treat the stated condition (e.g., antipsychotic for infection, anticoagulant for TIA already anticoagulated, brand/generic confusion); (2) safety/interaction neglect — ignoring that the patient is already on a drug whose class overlaps with the proposed option, or that a proposed option interacts with a current medicine; (3) evidence overreach — inferring a diagnosis or contraindication not actually stated, or treating a brand name as a different drug from its generic.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the working diagnosis and the mechanism of action each option would need. Eliminate any option whose primary indication does not match the stated condition, even if it is otherwise safe. Only then compare remaining options on patient-specific factors."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_class_overlap",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine, identify its drug class and therapeutic goal. Reject any option that duplicates that class or mechanism unless the case explicitly states a reason to add it (e.g., dual therapy is indicated). Adding a same-class agent without a stated rationale is a safety failure, not a benefit."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check_stated_only",
      "text": "Check interactions only between the proposed option and medicines the patient is explicitly stated to be taking. If a known interaction exists between the option and a current medicine, prefer an alternative that treats the same indication without that interaction. Do not invent interactions for medicines not mentioned."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Treat only explicitly stated patient facts as present. Do not assume a diagnosis, allergy, lab value, or contraindication that the dialogue does not state. If a fact needed to choose safely is missing, prefer the option that is indicated for the stated condition and has no stated conflict, rather than assuming the missing fact."
    },
    {
      "id": "brand_generic_equivalence",
      "text": "Recognize brand and generic names of the same active drug as one medication. When two options are the same molecule, they are not distinct choices; select based on the other options' indication and safety, and do not treat the brand as a separate therapeutic entity."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_to_indication_mapping",
      "text": "Map the patient's stated symptom cluster to the indicated drug class before looking at specific options. Infection signs (fever, night sweats, productive cough, skin boils) point to antimicrobials; focal neurologic deficit points to antiplatelet/anticoagulant or vascular therapy; inflammatory nerve pain points to anti-inflammatory agents. Use this mapping to filter options, then apply safety checks."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication match",
    "drug class overlap",
    "current medication",
    "interaction",
    "brand generic",
    "stated facts only"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or symptom cluster, every current medicine with its class and purpose, and any explicitly stated allergies, labs, or contraindications. Do not infer unstated facts.",
    "safety_check": "Screen each candidate against current medicines for class duplication and known interactions, using only stated facts. Flag same-class add-ons and interacting agents as unsafe unless the case gives an explicit reason.",
    "option_selection": "First filter by indication match to the stated condition, then by absence of class overlap or interaction with current medicines, then by brand/generic equivalence. Choose the remaining option that best fits both indication and stated safety profile."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication mismatch — selecting a drug that does not treat the diagnosed condition (e.g., choosing a non-antifungal for tinea, a corticosteroid-sparing agent over first-line steroid, or a non-indicated agent for anemia); (2) safety/contraindication weighting — over- or under-weighting a comorbidity or current medicine relative to the primary indication (e.g., avoiding a first-line drug for a documented condition because of a secondary concern, or ignoring that a listed comorbidity is controlled); (3) first-line vs. alternative confusion — preferring a second-line, systemic, or non-outpatient option when a standard first-line agent is available and not contraindicated. The agent needs explicit procedures for matching drug class to diagnosis, ranking first-line therapy, and applying only documented contraindications.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_match",
      "text": "Before selecting, restate the working diagnosis and the drug class that treats it. Eliminate any option whose primary indication does not match the diagnosis (e.g., antipsychotics for sarcoidosis, anticoagulants for anemia, diabetes drugs for anxiety). Only then compare remaining options on safety and line of therapy."
    },
    {
      "id": "first_line_preference",
      "text": "When multiple options are indicated, prefer the standard first-line agent for the diagnosed condition over second-line, rescue, or non-outpatient alternatives. Do not downgrade a first-line drug merely because a second-line option also appears on the list."
    },
    {
      "id": "documented_contraindication_only",
      "text": "Apply a contraindication or interaction only when the patient's stated condition or current medicine is documented in the dialogue. A controlled or historical condition (e.g., treated ulcer, controlled Raynaud's) is not an active contraindication unless the dialogue says it is currently active or uncontrolled. Do not invent missing facts."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_weighting",
      "text": "When a comorbidity could worsen with a first-line drug, weigh the severity and control status stated by the patient. If the comorbidity is described as controlled or historical, do not let it override the indicated first-line therapy; if it is active and directly worsened by the drug's mechanism, prefer a safer indicated alternative."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_setting_fit",
      "text": "Match the option to the care setting and route implied by the case. Prefer topical or oral outpatient therapy for localized conditions (e.g., topical antifungal for tinea corporis) and avoid inpatient-only or parenteral agents (e.g., midazolam) unless the setting requires them."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_check",
      "text": "List the patient's current medicines and check each candidate against them for clinically significant interactions. If no interaction is documented or expected, do not exclude an otherwise indicated first-line drug on interaction grounds."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "first-line therapy",
    "indication match",
    "documented contraindication",
    "controlled comorbidity",
    "route of administration",
    "drug interaction"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the working diagnosis, the specific drug class indicated for it, all current medicines, and each comorbidity with its stated control status. Mark facts as present only if explicitly stated; treat unstated facts as unknown.",
    "safety_check": "Check only documented contraindications and interactions. Distinguish active/uncontrolled conditions from controlled or historical ones. Do not exclude a first-line drug for a controlled comorbidity or an undocumented interaction.",
    "option_selection": "First eliminate options whose indication does not match the diagnosis. Among indicated options, prefer the standard first-line agent and the route/setting-appropriate form. Use safety only to break ties among indicated options, not to override a clearly indicated first-line drug without a documented active contraindication."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) brand-vs-generic equivalence is not recognized, so the same active drug is treated as a distinct option and the wrong one is chosen; (2) the stated diagnosis is not mapped to the specific drug class or mechanism that treats it, allowing unrelated options to be selected; (3) patient-specific safety facts (hepatic, renal, interaction, age/population) are either ignored or over-weighted, causing a safer or more appropriate alternative to be missed. A fourth, smaller mechanism is failure to prefer the option whose route/formulation matches the infection or condition (topical vs systemic).",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "brand_generic_equivalence",
      "text": "When two options are the same active ingredient under different names (brand and generic), treat them as one therapeutic choice. Do not select between them on the basis of the name alone; if the active drug is indicated, either name is acceptable, and the decision should rest on the drug's indication and safety, not on brand recognition."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_mechanism_match",
      "text": "First identify the stated condition and the drug class or mechanism that treats it. Eliminate any option whose mechanism does not address that condition (e.g., antifungal for a cardiovascular problem, diuretic for thrombosis, antidepressant for a fungal infection). Only then compare the remaining mechanism-appropriate options on patient-specific safety."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient_safety_weighting",
      "text": "Extract every stated patient fact that could affect drug choice: organ function (hepatic, renal), prior drug reactions, current medicines, age/population (e.g., premature infant), and relevant family history. Use these to rank mechanism-appropriate options. A history of elevated liver enzymes or renal impairment should shift preference toward an agent with less hepatic or renal handling, but a minor or resolved abnormality should not automatically exclude an otherwise indicated drug. Do not invent facts that were not stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check",
      "text": "When the patient is taking a current medicine, check whether it interacts with or duplicates the mechanism of each candidate. A current antiplatelet does not substitute for anticoagulation when anticoagulation is indicated; a current antidepressant does not contraindicate an antifungal unless a specific interaction exists. Only avoid an option when a real, stated interaction or contraindication is present."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_formulation_fit",
      "text": "Match the route and formulation of the option to the condition. For localized superficial infections, prefer a topical agent over a systemic one when both are mechanism-appropriate. For systemic or severe conditions, prefer the systemic option. Do not choose a systemic drug for a superficial problem or a topical drug for a systemic problem when a better-matched route exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "population_specific_choice",
      "text": "When the patient is a special population (e.g., premature infant, older adult, pregnant patient), prefer the option that is established and appropriate for that population. Do not select an option intended for a different population or a different condition merely because it shares a drug class."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "brand vs generic",
    "active ingredient",
    "indication match",
    "mechanism of action",
    "hepatic impairment",
    "renal impairment",
    "drug interaction",
    "topical vs systemic",
    "special population"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, the current medicines, and all relevant patient facts including organ function, prior reactions, age/population, and family history. Distinguish stated facts from absent ones; never treat a missing fact as present.",
    "safety_check": "Check each candidate against the stated patient facts for contraindications, interactions, and organ-function concerns. Apply safety weighting only to facts that were actually stated, and do not exclude an indicated drug for a minor or resolved abnormality unless a real contraindication exists.",
    "option_selection": "First eliminate options whose mechanism does not treat the stated condition. Then, among mechanism-appropriate options, prefer the one that best fits the patient's stated safety profile, current medicines, route/formulation needs, and population. Treat brand and generic names of the same active ingredient as a single choice."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights salient but non-decisive patient facts (family history, allergy labels, symptom overlap) and under-weights the core indication match, first-line status, and the distinction between documented personal contraindications versus vague or irrelevant history. It also fails to prefer safer within-class alternatives when a plausible but less appropriate option is present.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, state the working diagnosis and the accepted first-line drug class or mechanism for it. Eliminate any option whose primary indication does not match that diagnosis, even if it treats a symptom or a comorbid condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "personal_vs_family_risk",
      "text": "Treat only documented personal contraindications, allergies, or current interacting medicines as hard safety exclusions. A vague family history, a patient's worry, or a historical condition that is not active should not eliminate an otherwise indicated first-line option; instead, note it as a monitoring consideration."
    },
    {
      "id": "within_class_safety",
      "text": "When several options share the same indication, prefer the one with the best safety profile for this patient: avoid dependence-forming agents for chronic non-severe conditions, avoid agents that can worsen an active problem, and prefer agents without cross-reactivity to a documented allergy."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_vs_disease_treatment",
      "text": "Distinguish treatments that address the underlying disease from those that only relieve symptoms or treat a secondary problem. Choose disease-directed therapy when the diagnosis is established, and reserve symptomatic or supportive agents for when no disease-directed option is appropriate."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_interaction",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine, check whether any candidate option interacts with it, duplicates its effect, or is contraindicated alongside it. Do not assume an interaction exists without a stated mechanism; do not ignore a stated temporal association between a current drug and the presenting symptoms."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Do not treat a missing patient fact as present, and do not infer a contraindication from an absent history. If a safety fact is unknown, state that it is unknown and choose the option that is safest under uncertainty rather than assuming the worst."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "first-line",
    "indication match",
    "personal contraindication",
    "family history",
    "within-class safety",
    "dependence risk",
    "symptomatic vs disease-directed",
    "current medication interaction",
    "unknown safety fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the working diagnosis, the current medicines, documented allergies, and active comorbidities. Label each fact as personal/documented, family/historical, or unknown. Do not upgrade a vague or historical fact into an active personal contraindication.",
    "safety_check": "Apply hard exclusions only for documented personal contraindications, allergies, or current interacting medicines. Treat family history and inactive conditions as monitoring considerations, not exclusions. If a safety fact is missing, mark it unknown and prefer the safer option under uncertainty.",
    "option_selection": "First match each option to the working diagnosis and its accepted first-line class. Eliminate non-indicated options. Among remaining indicated options, prefer the one with the best patient-specific safety profile, avoiding dependence-forming, symptom-worsening, or allergy-cross-reactive agents. Distinguish disease-directed from purely symptomatic treatment."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication mismatch — the chosen option treats a different organ system or disease than the one the clinician actually diagnosed, because the agent anchored on a salient symptom (pain, swelling, dizziness) instead of the stated diagnosis; (2) safety/contraindication neglect — pregnancy, anticoagulation, or allergy history was not used to exclude an option, and in one case a missing fact (no explicit contraindication) was treated as permission; (3) formulation/route and severity mismatch — an injectable or high-potency agent was preferred over an oral first-line agent for a chronic/outpatient problem, and a symptomatic-only agent was preferred over a disease-modifying one.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_anchor",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the clinician's working diagnosis in one sentence and require every candidate to have an indication for that diagnosis. If an option treats only a symptom (e.g., pain, fever, urinary burning) or a different organ system than the stated diagnosis, rank it below options that treat the underlying condition. Do not let a salient symptom override the stated diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_specificity",
      "text": "When two options share a drug class, prefer the one whose approved indication matches the diagnosed condition and the patient's severity/setting. Prefer oral, first-line, disease-modifying therapy for chronic outpatient conditions over injectable, high-potency, or symptom-only alternatives unless the case states a reason (e.g., inability to take oral, acute severe flare) that requires the alternative."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_gate",
      "text": "Before finalizing, screen every candidate against stated patient facts: pregnancy/lactation, anticoagulation, allergy history, renal/hepatic function, and current medicines. Exclude any option contraindicated by a stated fact. A fact that is absent is not evidence of safety — do not treat 'no contraindication stated' as clearance; instead prefer the option with the fewest unresolved safety questions."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check",
      "text": "For each candidate, check for interactions with the patient's current medicines and conditions. If an option has a plausible interaction or additive risk with a stated medicine (e.g., anticoagulant, antihypertensive, asthma controller), downgrade it relative to an option with no such interaction, and state the interaction explicitly rather than assuming it is absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "pregnancy_guard",
      "text": "If the patient is pregnant or possibly pregnant, treat pregnancy as a hard safety filter: exclude options with known fetal risk or uncertain pregnancy safety, and prefer options established as safe in pregnancy for the diagnosed condition. Do not select an option merely because it is mentioned as a possible treatment in the dialogue."
    },
    {
      "id": "evidence_tiebreak",
      "text": "When indication and safety do not separate two options, break the tie using: (1) match to the specific diagnosed condition rather than a related one, (2) route/formulation appropriate to the care setting, (3) lower risk of dependence, toxicity, or monitoring burden, and (4) first-line status in standard guidance. Record the tiebreak reason so the choice is reproducible."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "working diagnosis",
    "indication match",
    "contraindication",
    "pregnancy safety",
    "drug interaction",
    "first-line therapy",
    "route of administration",
    "symptomatic vs disease-modifying"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract and restate: the clinician's stated working diagnosis, the specific symptoms supporting it, current medicines, and all hard safety facts (pregnancy, anticoagulation, allergy history, renal/hepatic status). Distinguish stated facts from absent ones; never infer a missing fact.",
    "safety_check": "Apply a hard filter using only stated facts: exclude options contraindicated by pregnancy, anticoagulation, allergy history, or organ impairment. Treat absent facts as unknown, not safe. Flag interactions with current medicines and downgrade affected options.",
    "option_selection": "Rank options by (1) indication for the stated diagnosis, (2) absence of contraindications from stated facts, (3) absence of interactions, (4) route/formulation and first-line status appropriate to the setting. Prefer disease-modifying over symptom-only and oral first-line over injectable/high-potency unless the case justifies otherwise."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the most salient symptom or the option named in the dialogue, instead of first establishing the active problem and then applying indication plus patient-specific safety. Failures cluster into: (1) selecting a drug for a comorbidity rather than the primary active condition; (2) missing that the current medicine is the failing or interacting agent; (3) ignoring additive toxicity or contraindication from current medicines; (4) choosing an option with no indication for the stated problem; (5) treating a named-but-absent medicine as present.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "active_problem_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, state the single active problem the encounter is diagnosing or treating. Rank options by whether they are indicated for that active problem, not for a secondary or historical symptom. If an option treats only a comorbidity while another treats the active problem, prefer the active-problem option unless safety forbids it."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_failure_or_interaction",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine for the same condition, treat that medicine as potentially failing, not as the answer. If symptoms persist or worsen on the current drug, prefer an option with a different mechanism or a different drug class over re-selecting the same drug or a same-class alternative, unless the case explicitly supports dose optimization or continuation."
    },
    {
      "id": "additive_toxicity_and_contraindication",
      "text": "Check every option against current medicines for additive CNS depression, GI irritation, bleeding risk, organ toxicity, and class overlap. If an option shares a toxic mechanism with a current medicine, downgrade it even when it is otherwise indicated. Do not invent a contraindication that is not stated or implied by the drug class."
    },
